> These messages come up often on the list. The notes written in disk-io.c say
> this:
>   * we can't consider a given block up to date unless the transid of the
>   * block matches the transid in the parent node's pointer.  This is how we
>   * detect blocks that either didn't get written at all or got written
>   * in the wrong place.
>
> I don't know whether this definitely means hardware related problems of some
> sort, but it sounds suspiciously like that because blocks should get written in
> the correct place. Right? But they didn't.

While I am not too sure about the underlying harddrive myself, I would 
trust dm-crypt and LUKS. With the XTS disc encryption mode any bit error 
will result in a completely random 16byte block. Also any block (16byte 
AES block or 512byte disk block alike) that is written to the wrong 
place on the harddrive (i.e. on the ciphertext side) will be decrypted 
to a completely random block on the next read. I believe a random block 
of data in the btrfs fs would result in more drastic results than what I 
encounter?

